    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ed04054b-f381-58d6-a31b-da0c064785c9",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following system of equations.\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4p - 18q &=20 \\\\ -9p + 3q &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $q$ from the system:\nUse an integer multiplier and leave the other equation unchanged.\n\nTo eliminate $q$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that the coefficient of $q$ in the first equation is $-18$, while in the second equation it is $3$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by ${\\color{blue}6}$, then the coefficient of $q$ will turn into $18$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-18$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}&-4p - 18q = 20 \\\\ &(-9p + 3q = 3)\\cdot (6)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4p - 18q &=20 \\\\ -54p + 18q &=18\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $q$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4p - \\cancel{18q} &= 20 \\\\\n-54p + \\cancel{18q} &= 18 \\\\\n(-4p - 54p) &= (20 + 18) \\\\\n-58p &= 38\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $q$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{second}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}6}$, and add the equations*.",
